  "biography": {
    "plain_text": "Charles Rudolph Walgreen Jr. was an American pharmacist who succeeded his father in leading Walgreens. A University of Michigan graduate, he became president and later chairman of the family drugstore chain. He expanded its retail operations, promoted self-service shopping and shortened pharmacists' working hours as the business grew across the country.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 95,
      "band": "very_high",
      "reason": "Primary corporate obituary, independent business reporting and institutional record establish the career."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S1",
      "S2",
      "S6"
    ],
    "word_count": 51,
    "html": "<p>Charles Rudolph Walgreen Jr. was an American pharmacist who succeeded his father in leading Walgreens. A University of Michigan graduate, he became president and later chairman of the family drugstore chain. He expanded its retail operations, promoted self-service shopping and shortened pharmacists' working hours as the business grew across the country.</p>\r\n"
  },
  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "At Chicago's International Museum of Surgical Science, Charles Walgreen Jr. helped preserve the history of his profession by funding a turn-of-the-century apothecary exhibit. The former pharmacy executive served on the museum's board for many years and contributed to an endowment intended to support its future activities.\n\nHis university giving extended beyond pharmacy. At the University of Michigan, he committed USD 10 million towards a drama centre that would house the Arthur Miller Theatre. The project also provided rehearsal rooms, teaching space and a scene shop. Other gifts supported scholarships and endowed teaching posts, including work on reading and literacy.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 95,
      "band": "very_high",
      "reason": "Recipient institutions directly document the exhibit, endowment, drama-centre commitment and wider educational support."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S4",
      "S5",
      "S1"
    ],
    "word_count": 99,
    "html": "<p>At Chicago's International Museum of Surgical Science, Charles Walgreen Jr. helped preserve the history of his profession by funding a turn-of-the-century apothecary exhibit. The former pharmacy executive served on the museum's board for many years and contributed to an endowment intended to support its future activities.</p>\r\n<p>His university giving extended beyond pharmacy. At the University of Michigan, he committed USD 10 million towards a drama centre that would house the Arthur Miller Theatre. The project also provided rehearsal rooms, teaching space and a scene shop. Other gifts supported scholarships and endowed teaching posts, including work on reading and literacy.</p>\r\n"
  },
